{
  "summary": "The prior rounds have converged well on the three-tier strategy but continue to undercount the actual extraction surface and overstate the simplicity of the 'revised Tier 1.' The five 'genuinely pure' functions total ~190 lines — a 4.5% reduction of a 4,252-line file — which is too marginal to justify the extraction overhead as a standalone commit unless it's explicitly framed as scaffolding for later tiers. More critically, the discussion keeps treating _build_feature_detail_widgets as the only impure function in the top section, but the code at lines 310-483 also reveals a pervasive pattern of inline `import re` and repeated manual Rich-markup escaping (replace '[', replace '{', re.sub for '%') that appears at least 8 times across _build_feature_detail_widgets alone. Before any extraction, this duplication should be consolidated into _escape_markup itself (which currently misses '{'/'}' escaping), which would shrink _build_feature_detail_widgets by ~30 lines and make the extraction boundary cleaner. The third contributor's suggestion to start Tier 2 with 'the 3-4 simplest action_* methods' is sound in principle but the actual code shows the modal cancel actions (there are 8 separate action_cancel methods on different ModalScreen subclasses at lines 631, 688, 942, 993, 1067, 1143, 1191, 1325) are far simpler candidates than action_promote/action_restore — they're one-liners that just dismiss the modal. These don't need a command-pattern extraction at all and could be consolidated via a shared base class, which is Tier 3 modal extraction work that's been consistently deprioritized despite being lower-risk than the @work Tier 2 methods. The sequencing should be: (1) fix _escape_markup to handle all Rich metacharacters and deduplicate inline escaping, (2) extract pure helpers, (3) introduce a BaseModal with shared action_cancel/styling, (4) then tackle action_* command pattern for PipelineApp methods. For _check_for_changes, the third contributor correctly identified that mtime caching is already present but _load_features re-parses every JSON — however the proposed fix of caching FeatureFile objects by (path, mtime) needs to account for the fact that FeatureFile.list_all() at line 2636 loads features across ALL boards for server push, not just the active board, so the cache must be global not per-board."
}
